# BMS Molecular Translation — solution

Task: predict the InChI string of a chemical structure image; metric = **mean Levenshtein distance** (lower is better).

## Final approach: pretrained OCSR on confident rows + our own image→InChI model elsewhere

1. **Primary (MolScribe, general pretrained OCSR):**
   - Run `MolScribe` (image → molecular graph/SMILES) at 384 px on every test image.
   - Convert SMILES → InChI with RDKit, with cleanup (`*` dummy atoms removed, largest fragment kept) and a formula-layer fallback.
   - Keep the MolScribe InChI **only when it parses in RDKit AND its decoder confidence ≥ 0.5**.
2. **Fallback (our own model, trained only on the provided training set):**
   - ConvNeXt-tiny (ImageNet `convnext_tiny.fb_in22k_ft_in1k`) encoder + 6-layer Transformer decoder, BPE-1024 tokenizer over InChI characters, 384×384 grayscale→RGB input, trained on `data/train_labels.csv` (public train only) with bf16, label smoothing and cosine LR.
   - Used on every row not accepted in step 1 (~20% + low-confidence rows). The model's **raw** InChI string is used (it is NOT required to be RDKit-valid — raw strings are much closer on hard images).
3. Assemble `submission.csv` (`image_id,InChI`).

The threshold (0.5) and the rule were chosen on a **holdout cut from the training set only** (`analysis/holdout_20k.csv`; tuning subset = first 2,000 rows). No test labels were ever used.

## Validation (holdout, mean Levenshtein)
| method | mean Lev |
|---|---|
| MolScribe (1m) + cleanup | ~39.8 |
| MolScribe 1m→680k validity gate | ~35.2 |
| rotation-TTA of MolScribe on failed rows | ~32.8 |
| our model alone (final ckpt) | see board |
| **final rule (MolScribe-valid&conf≥0.5 else our model)** | **see board** |

## External models / libraries (sources & licenses)
- **MolScribe** — model id `yujieq/MolScribe` on Hugging Face (https://huggingface.co/yujieq/MolScribe), revision `a0189776b7415b82795c7ee81eed311bf5c8724b`, license **MIT**. Checkpoint used: `swin_base_char_aux_1m.pth` (image→chemical-structure). General-purpose pretrained OCSR model; **not** trained on this competition's data/labels and not a competition solution. Code loaded via the official `molscribe` package (PyPI `MolScribe==1.1.1`, official repo https://github.com/thomas0809/MolScribe) together with `OpenNMT-py==2.2.0` and `SmilesPE==0.0.3`, installed into an isolated directory (base python env not modified).
- **RDKit** — SMILES↔InChI conversion and validity checks (BSD-3-Clause).
- **timm / torchvision** — ImageNet-pretrained ConvNeXt-tiny backbone (`convnext_tiny.fb_in22k_ft_in1k`, Apache-2.0 / model-card terms) for our own model.
- **HuggingFace tokenizers** — BPE tokenizer trained on the provided InChI labels only.

All pretrained weights are general-purpose (ImageNet / general chemical OCR). No competition-specific checkpoints, notebooks, write-ups or leaderboards were used.

## Code layout (`code/`)
- `molscribe_infer.py` — MolScribe sharded inference on test images (`image_id,smiles,score`).
- `postproc.py` — SMILES→InChI cleanup + formula fallback + Levenshtein.
- `infer_rigel.py`, `model.py`, `train.py` — our image→InChI model (inference / architecture / training).
- `finalize_rigel.py` — final assembly: `1m valid & score≥thr ? MolScribe : our model`.
- `validate_submission.py` — coverage/format/RDKit validation of `submission.csv`.

## Reproduce
1. `python code/molscribe_infer.py --ids test_ids.csv --imgroot data/test --out preds/shardN.csv --shard N --nshard 4 --conf`
2. postprocess (`code/postproc.py`) → `preds1m_inchi.csv` (`image_id,inchi,valid,score`).
3. build `rigel_infer_ids.csv` = rows with `not valid OR score < 0.7`.
4. `python code/infer_rigel.py --ckpt <ckpt> --ids_csv rigel_infer_ids.csv --imgroot data/test --out rigel_test.csv`
5. `python code/finalize_rigel.py --inchi1 preds1m_inchi.csv --rigel rigel_test.csv --score_thr 0.5 --out final_solution/submission.csv`
